plan Machines
title   Machines: definitions, fragments, states, turns, guards, schedules, activities and reactions
status  proposed
goal
    An author declares an actor's states and events once. Each accepted event produces one
    consistent result; guards, views and tests agree about what the actor can do.

    **Why now:** everything that runs or reads an actor depends on one meaning for its states and events, so the
    declaration and the turn algorithm are fixed once, here.

    **Read first:** @spec.Compile and @spec.Turn, the Specs built from this Plan.
outcomes
    O1  Definitions, fragments and machines
        Declaring a definition or machine starts nothing, and an invalid declaration throws one FlowError listing every
        violation found.

        definition(config) and machine(definition, callback) return synchronously and start no
        actors, work, timers or storage. machine() is the compile: its output is plain data plus
        named closures, booted by the Runtime in the same process (@plan.Runtime.O1).

        Invalid construction throws one FlowError containing all discovered violations in
        declaration then rule order, with offending field paths. The callback runs exactly once with
        { S, E, O } and returns static machine configuration.

        A definition requires id, states and events, and may declare `internalEvents`, memory,
        traits, operations, context and persist. persist defaults to false; persist: true on the
        definition makes the machine's shared actors persisted (@plan.Persistence.O2). states is an
        ordered list of leaf names, single-key compounds containing child lists, or { NAME: Sub }
        placing a subroutine; a node that carries traits is wrapped as Flow.state(children | {} |
        Sub, { traits }). events and optional `internalEvents` are records from event names to
        Schema field records; {} means no payload.

        A payload may instead be a Schema.Struct with checks; a payload failing its check is refused
        at admission, before guards run.

        An internal event may be marked Flow.uncertain(fields); every unknown: target of an
        operation is such an event or Flow.ignore (@plan.Operations.O3), otherwise
        UnknownNotUncertain at construction.

        Memory is a record of Schema fields; a field's start is written on the field. Flow.input(s)
        is supplied by the creator, and the definition's Input type is exactly these fields;
        Flow.initial(s, value) is a constant start and Flow.initial(s, ({ ...fields }) => value) a
        pure computed start from input or constant fields, never from another computed field;
        Flow.private(s) is seen only by its owner.

        Every field starts from exactly one source: Flow.input, Flow.initial, or absence for an
        optional key; a field with none is a type error, and Flow.input and Flow.initial never share
        a field. A field has one default, the same at creation and restore; two different defaults,
        or a Schema default beside Flow.initial, are TwoDefaults, found from the Schema's structure.

        A default is pure: a default whose Effect is not a succeeded value (a clock read, a fresh id
        per call) is a construction error, ImpureDefault (proposed name, the runner's), found by
        reading the Schema without running it; a time or an id comes from an event or the Runtime's
        mint. Flow is outermost: a Schema modifier over a Flow marker is a type error.

        Creation is pure and runs once per incarnation: it checks a present input by the Input
        Schema's type side, with no transforming decode, copies the input fields, applies constant
        then computed initials, and reads no clock or randomness, because it also runs in render
        (@plan.React.O2). It never runs on restore, adoption or hydration.

        Input fields are ordinary memory afterwards. A machine's input fields are read-only (writing
        one is a type error), since they name the actor (@plan.Runtime.O3); on a value-named machine
        they are required, default-free and string-encoded, else IdentityFieldOptional (proposed
        name).

        A fragment's input fields stay writable. At restore a field the record lacks takes its
        default or computed initial, an optional key stays absent, and an input field with no
        default refuses the record (RestoreMissingInput); record versions, resets and dropped fields
        are @plan.Persistence.O5.

        Optional context maps slots to another definition's Provider.select(selector); context
        binding is @plan.Runtime.O5. operations is ({ E }) => ({ name: contract.outcomes({ ... }) |
        contract }): each server interaction is declared once by name with its endings mapped onto
        this definition's events (@plan.Operations.O1 to O3); one write operation per write contract
        per actor, counting every fragment placement in its tree (SharedContract, proposed name,
        otherwise).

        The definition exports O, the operations' handles; a commit's or lookup's select is typed at
        its transition. Machine configuration mirrors the declared state tree exactly: root and
        compounds require a default direct-child token and states; leaves have neither.

        Nodes may declare on, activities and schedules, and fill each placed fragment in one block
        (traits under traits.name where placed, a subroutine on its state); only the root may
        declare `onContext`. A definition's handler(path, h) and guard(path?, g) type shared code as
        if written at path (the root when omitted).

        Fragments. trait(declaration, body) is something the actor has; subroutine(declaration,
        body) is a state the actor goes into.

        A fragment declares id, events, `hostEvents` (raised only by its direct host),
        `internalEvents` (from its own schedules or operation endings), guards (Flow.Guard),
        callbacks (Flow.Callback), exits (Flow.Exit, subroutines only), memory, child traits,
        operations and, for a subroutine, states and default; ports are required unless wrapped in
        Flow.optional (an optional guard allows, an optional callback ignores). Its body holds on,
        schedules, activities, its children's fillings and `whenAbandoned` (notified when an
        incarnation ends other than by its own exit or a freeze).

        Each fragment carries an inferred summary (per event: callbacks notified, exits taken,
        totality; whenAbandoned's callbacks; its and its children's write operations and their
        Effect requirements), part of its type; hosts are checked against summaries, never bodies.
        Fragments may not declare context, `onContext`, send or persist.

        Placement:

        - the root's traits, a node's Flow.state traits, or a subroutine state;
        - T.resumable() freezes instead of dropping when its node is left;
        - T.each({ of, max, by? }) places one copy per key of a host memory field.

        A `resetOn` filling returns a reset value compared by Equal.equals; null or undefined means
        the trait is not placed (becoming absent ends the incarnation and runs whenAbandoned,
        becoming present starts one), and the filling's parts receive key, the present reset value
        (the copy key under .each).

        Trait instance names, operation names, state names and memory keys share one namespace; a
        collision rejects; key and item are reserved memory names in a trait placed with .each.

        A fragment's operations map onto its own `internalEvents`. A fragment may own a write that
        maps anything only if every placement from the actor's root to it is a root trait, an .each
        copy or a .resumable() trait, any of which may carry `resetOn`, with no subroutine on the
        path; there a `resetOn` must return a string, null or undefined.

        Elsewhere it may own reads, streams and bare writes. Acting members (commit, lookup,
        subscribe, retry, cancel, cancelAll, refetch) are typed only in the declaring fragment;
        outside it Fragment.O offers only its write operations' reads (get in views; get, idle and
        retryable in a host's guards and updateMemory).

        A key-less O.x.cancel listed in `whenAbandoned` releases that operation's lanes of the
        ending incarnation on leave (@plan.Operations.O7).

        Unknown fields, wrong kinds, empty compounds, duplicate siblings, overlapping public and
        internal event names, _tag payload/input/memory fields, self-selecting context and invalid
        operation handles reject. State names is and child, top-level root, and the names self and
        __proto__ (ReservedStateName) are reserved.

        State paths have at most ten segments. Authored names and ids are nonempty, at most 256
        UTF-8 bytes, contain no C0 controls, DEL or lone surrogates, and retain exact spelling: no
        Unicode normalization or locale comparison.

        Construction errors also include unfilled required ports, a raise into a fragment the turn's
        state change exits, two state changers or overlapping keys that state-token pruning cannot
        separate, an acting fragment member used outside its fragment, and a mapped write or
        `resetOn` outside the fragment write scope above; the full finding list is
        @plan.CommandLine.O5.

        The machine cannot add states, events or operations. Foreign tokens, undeclared handler
        events, foreign producer events, non-public send events and duplicate schedule names
        reject. Targets, operations, outcome events and send/write targets are static; callbacks
        compute values only. Starting a write is static: only by listing a commit in actions.

        Definition literals, payloads, context, operation names and state paths infer without
        assertions, explicit generics or wrappers; invalid machine additions are also type errors.
        Definitions expose Event, State, Input, Memory and Snapshot Schemas.

        Event decodes only public _tag envelopes; State accepts complete leaf paths. E.X(payload)
        validates on every call and throws InvalidEvent on mismatch; payload-free constructors take
        no argument. Persisted-value eligibility is @plan.Persistence.O2.
    O2  States and transitions
        Exactly one leaf is active. Substates share the actor's memory, context and lifetime;
        reaching a leaf with no handlers does not complete or dispose the actor. S.OPEN.EDITING
        names that state; token.is(snapshot or state) includes descendants and narrows types.

        A state token may be an element of a guard array; it passes when the pre-turn state is at or
        below it, and flow check reads it structurally. token.child(snapshot) returns its active
        direct child's name, otherwise undefined. S.root.child(snapshot) returns the active
        top-level state. S.X.history targets X's last active child, or its default, and
        S.X.history.child(s) reads it; history restores control only, and data comes back only
        through placement or .resumable().

        on maps an event name to a token, { target?, guard?, updateMemory?, actions?, raise?,
        notify? }, or an ordered array of transitions. target is this machine's token or "self"; an
        omitted target means "self". The active leaf's handler is tried first, then ancestors up to
        the root.

        At each level, the first passing or unguarded alternative wins; all declining falls through
        to the next ancestor. With no enabled transition, nothing changes and no work starts. A
        child's refusal inside a selected alternative never falls through (@O3).

        "self" changes no state, restarts no work and keeps the activation, including at the root.
        An explicit target re-enters itself if active, as a new activation, even when it names the
        handler's own state, so a call the old activation owns ends late (@plan.Operations.O3);
        targeting an active ancestor re-enters it and its descendants through its defaults.

        For a handler at H targeting T, the unchanged boundary is H when T is a strict descendant of
        H; otherwise it is the nearest proper ancestor of T that also contains H. Active states
        below that boundary exit deepest first; the path to T and its defaults enters outermost
        first.

        Work at and above that boundary survives unchanged. Parents' activities start before
        children's and stop after them. Defaults reach a leaf in one publication with no
        intermediate work or snapshot.

        A subroutine state is entered at its default, or is a leaf. It is left by an exit, or
        abandoned by a transition that exits it; host events fall through to its ancestors as
        usual, and an ancestor's "self" does not abandon it. Exits are targets only fragments take;
        an exit filling must leave the subroutine's state, and every exit is filled. A callback
        never moves the host out of the fragment's placement node.
    O3  Turns
        Each accepted event, with everything it raises, notifies, exits, resets and leaves, compiles into one turn that
        the runtime and check() run by the same algorithm.

        Input ordering and atomic actor/store publication are @plan.Runtime.O4. The turn algorithm
        is normative for the runtime and for check(): admit, select the source, select the parts,
        seed and compute, validate, commit. One transition and everything it raises, notifies,
        exits, resets and leaves compile into one turn.

        Admit (runtime only): a pending reset from the last commit runs first as its own follow-up
        turn; otherwise the next event. A call's ending is mapped here through its operation's
        outcomes, its lane cell applied, and its owner tested against the pre-turn snapshot
        (@plan.Operations.O3); a timer of a dead activation is discarded; firings with the same
        deadline are separate turns.

        Select the source: from the active leaf, or for a late ending the ancestor its owner test
        names, try alternatives in order, state-token elements first.

        A node with no written handler for an event but an active schedule listing it in `armOn` or
        `disarmOn` handles it with an implicit unguarded { target: "self" }. If every alternative
        declines, continue at the next ancestor; after the root the event is Denied (a handler
        existed) or Unhandled, and nothing of the turn's own changes, though an ending admitted at
        step one keeps its lane effects and counts as delivered.

        Select the parts: walk raises, the child transitions they select, the notifies and fillings
        these reach, and exits, depth first in written order; every selected alternative fixes its
        target. Before the walk `resetOn` runs and changed traits get a new incarnation, or end if
        absent. If an addressed child has no handler or all its alternatives decline, the part
        refuses the whole turn.

        After the walk each .each field's final list decides membership, and `whenAbandoned` runs
        for every incarnation the turn ends, fused into the leaving turn. Validate: list repair
        (never refuses), one state changer among the parts, disjoint memory keys, the after memory,
        a valid action batch, then each write member's lane cell in action order and the turn's
        releases.

        Commit: exits deepest first and entries outermost first, publish the after memory, cancel or
        freeze ended incarnations' schedules, arm schedules, re-evaluate `resetOn` into pending
        resets, queue adoption deliveries of started incarnations, publish once.

        Two memory views. Before, the pre-turn snapshot, is read by every guard, every payload
        mapper (raise, notify, exits, whenAbandoned) and every `updateMemory`.

        After, the memory the turn publishes (new incarnations' initial memory, then every patch in
        expansion order, after list repair), is read by work that only starts or times things:
        commit and lookup selects, member key selects, schedule functions, keys, `restartOn`,
        placement selects and when, and optimistic (captured at start). `resetOn` and input fillers
        read the before-walk memory (the pre-turn memory with the source's patch and earlier
        resets).

        Lane reads (operations, get, idle, retryable) in guards, selects and `updateMemory` see the
        pre-turn lanes with the turn's own ending admitted; keys, when, placement selects and
        published rows see the lanes with this turn's members applied. Each Partial<Memory> patch
        shallow-merges once. Only accepted transitions change memory. Updates, action parameters and
        send mappers receive now, one Runtime Clock epoch millisecond value shared throughout the
        turn; guards receive no time.

        One invalid action, a failing after-memory select or an invalid duration rejects the whole
        turn like a pre-commit defect: prior state and memory stay, no action runs, and one
        issue-only snapshot names the code; check() reports it as Denied with code InvalidTurn.

        A value that cannot be encoded never rejects a turn (@plan.Operations.O3). After
        publication, exited work releases, entered work starts, then finite actions start or join,
        invoked after the turn. Unhandled or denied events publish nothing, change no revision and
        start no work; denials may be observed in evidence without a revision.

        A pre-commit callback defect preserves prior state, memory, context and store truth, starts
        no work and publishes one issue-only snapshot naming the callback and path. It is not
        retried for that input. This includes guards, updates, selects, keys, send payloads,
        schedules, `restartOn`, placement selects and when predicates.

        A post-commit mapper, placeholder, optimistic-data or write callback defect publishes one
        issue, enqueues no event and applies no partial write batch; it neither rolls back the
        committed state nor retries that occurrence. Write semantics are @plan.Operations.O5.

        Outside callers can send only valid public event envelopes; invalid payloads or internal
        events fail with InvalidEvent before a turn exists. The same snapshot, event, Clock and
        seeded test randomness produce the same result without consulting host wall time.
    O4  Guards, deny and check
        Guards decide from the pre-turn snapshot and start no work, so views can trust check.

        A guard receives { event, state, memory, context, schedules, operations } from the pre-turn
        snapshot and returns true or deny(code, context?). code is a literal string; context is
        optional JSON-safe explanatory data. deny returns plain data matching Flow.Denial, so a
        denial may be stored in memory and a guard may return a stored one. false is a type error;
        any invalid return is a guard defect.

        Guards read only these captured inputs and start no work, so views can trust check. A guard
        slot may hold an array; all must pass. An operation's idle(k?) (a partial key passes only
        when every lane it matches is Idle) and retryable(k?, { times }) are guards
        (@plan.Operations.O3); O.x.get(operations, k) reads a row.

        A host reads a fragment's write lanes with Fragment.O.x.get, idle and retryable; every
        `updateMemory` receives operations (pre-turn lanes, get only); inside a fragment operations
        is its own rows.

        check(snapshot, event) runs the turn's selection, parts and validation without updating
        memory, admitting events, publishing, recording evidence, minting, joining queues,
        predicting, marking releases or starting work. It returns { _tag: "Allowed" } if a
        transition would run; { _tag: "Denied", reasons } if handlers exist but all decline, or the
        turn would be rejected (code InvalidTurn); or { _tag: "Unhandled" } if none exists.

        Denied reasons contain { code, context } for each declined alternative, deepest level then
        authored order; a declined state-token element reads { code: "NotInState", context: { state
        } }.

        A decline from another subject path is a verdict, like Rejected. Verdicts compare by
        Equal.equals. An old snapshot answers from its own captured values.

        A defective guard makes check throw GuardDefect without publishing an issue. Internal or
        foreign events are type errors and fail untyped calls with InvalidEvent.
    O5  Schedules
        A schedule fires an event on a declared timing, started and stopped by the events the machine lists.

        A node or fragment body declares schedules: { name: { schedule, send, armOn?, disarmOn?,
        restartOn?, keys? } }. schedule is a Duration.Input, an Effect Schedule, or ({ memory,
        context, now }) => either, reading the after memory of its arming turn with that turn's now.

        An invalid duration or schedule rejects the arming turn with the pre-commit defect behavior
        of @O3. send is this definition's payload-free event or E.X.from(metadata); metadata
        contains name, attempt (firings since arming, starting at 1), `armedAt`, `dueAt`, `firedAt`,
        output and, for a keyed schedule, key. Times use Runtime Clock milliseconds; Duration output
        is undefined. Armed schedules expose `dueAt` in the snapshot (@O8). An event mapped by an
        operation is never a schedule's send.

        A schedule without `armOn` starts on entry. armOn: [...] starts it on each accepted listed
        event, raised ones included, accepted anywhere on the active path, restarting it if running;
        with Flow.entry in the list it also starts on entry (its owner's state entered, trait
        created or reset, never a thaw), otherwise it is idle on entry. disarmOn: [events] stops it
        on each accepted listed event; stopping an idle schedule does nothing.

        Exit stops any schedule. An event in both lists, or `armOn` with `restartOn`, is a
        construction error. Every temporal construct is delayed: it never counts the turn that arms
        it. Descendant changes and "self" preserve deadlines. Each arming has an id: a firing from
        an earlier arming, or from an activation that ended before handling, is never delivered.

        A Duration fires once; a Schedule fires each step until complete. Equal deadlines enqueue by
        actor admission order, then outermost state, then declaration order, each a separate turn.
        An overdue step fires once, never in a burst; the next delay starts at actual firing.

        restartOn({ memory, context }) establishes a silent baseline from the activating turn's
        after memory and leaves the schedule idle on entry. After each committed turn, a changed
        selection under Equal.equals restarts only that schedule from its first step at commit time;
        equal values do nothing. A Duration returns to idle after firing, allowing debounce
        behavior.

        A keyed schedule's keys return key records from the after memory and after lanes (inside a
        fragment its own rows; under .each that copy's lanes): one timer per key, armed when the key
        appears, cancelled when it leaves, firing once with key; a repeated key keeps its first
        position with `listRepair` evidence; `restartOn` is evaluated per key, ({ key, memory }) =>
        value; `armOn` on a keyed schedule is a construction error.

        Per-key timers persist by key with `dueAt`, and on restore the restored memory's keys win.

        Schedules fire only while their actor is used (@plan.Runtime.O3). A deadline that comes due
        while nothing uses the actor waits, and only the latest arming waits (re-arming, disarming
        or exiting removes a waiting deadline).

        On restore, resume, a return after an end, or a use returning to an actor whose schedules
        waited, a Duration preserves its deadline and fires once if overdue, waiting deadlines in
        deadline order; a multi-step Schedule restarts at step one. A resumable trait's schedules
        keep `dueAt` while frozen and fire once on return if overdue. Restored `restartOn` baselines
        are silent. Hydration activation is @plan.ServerRendering.O5; deterministic time is
        @plan.Testing.O2.
    O6  Activities and outcomes in machines
        Operation activities are placed in states and traits, and their work is kept, replaced or released as the
        machine changes state.

        activities is a list of placements: O.x.subscribe(select, { when? }) or
        O.x.subscribe.each(select, { when? }) (one session per distinct key of the selected inputs,
        repaired like .each keys), declared in a state (a subroutine's states included) or a trait
        body and placed for that activation or incarnation. select and when read the after memory
        and after lanes; when may hold state tokens.

        Lane shapes and the placement's placeholder option are @plan.Operations.O6, streams
        @plan.Operations.O4. After each turn, an unchanged operation and key keep existing work; a
        changed key releases old work exactly once and starts new work; when false releases only
        that placement. A freeze releases a trait's placements and a thaw places them again.

        A live operation is placed in one state or trait incarnation at a time unless state tokens
        in when make placements exclusive (DuplicatePlacement); one operation uses one verb
        (VerbMismatch). A stream placement is one session per (actor incarnation, placement path,
        operation, key). Release failure reports once without blocking the transition.

        Endings map to events on the declarer, never at the call site: each operation's outcomes
        name the events (@plan.Operations.O2, O3, O4). Mapped events run in later ordinary turns.
        value fires only for a changed confirmed value under Equal.equals, never for predictions or
        optimistic values.

        Lane-only changes publish captured lanes without running handlers, guards, updates, actions,
        schedules, `restartOn` or `onContext`. Finite work starts only from an accepted transition's
        actions; operation action semantics are @plan.Operations.O2 and O3 and sending is
        @plan.Runtime.O4.

        A commit, lookup or retry is owned by the activation of the state its transition names
        ("self": the handler's own state). At admission its ending is current, routed from the
        active leaf, if that activation is alive; otherwise late, routed from the owner's nearest
        surviving strict ancestor; an adopted ending and every unknown { Restored } are current from
        the active leaf.

        In a late turn an alternative may not exit a state that owns other pending work, or an
        active subroutine (LateMayNotLeave): pending work is every call owned by an activation it
        exits, an InFlight head and every queued commit, behind a Held head too, whatever the
        transition cancels; a Held head is not.

        A late handler gives work up in an alternative that stays put, with `cancelAll` or a keyed
        cancel. flow check errors on a late handler with no alternative that stays put at the owner
        state or its ancestors; a late ending landing while the owner is not re-entered, at an
        ancestor whose alternatives all leave, is denied at run time, an accepted risk (the runner's
        ruling).

        Lateness is not transitive, and only the host's own operations count. Every late event that
        can occur is handled at a strict ancestor of its owner.
    O7  Context reactions
        A change in context can raise an event into an actor, and a reaction never changes state directly.

        Machine-level onContext: [{ select, when?, event }] observes context changes. select({
        context, memory }) establishes a silent baseline at admission, restore, adoption and
        hydration. Only context waves re-run it, using current memory; memory-only changes do not
        trigger it. Context propagation is @plan.Runtime.O5. Equal selections under Equal.equals are
        silent.

        A changed selection becomes the baseline even if when(current, previous) declines. With no
        when, or when true, event (bare or E.X.from(current)) enqueues after the context turn, in
        reaction declaration order; `onContext` events queue in the actor's one FIFO mailbox with
        user sends, call endings and timer firings (@plan.Runtime.O4).

        An actor returning after an end within a Runtime gets one wave if a provider's selection
        differs from the one it ended with; a reload's baselines are silent. A select, when or
        mapper defect publishes one issue, keeps the previous baseline and emits nothing; the next
        wave may try again. Reactions never change state directly.
    O8  The snapshot
        A snapshot is deeply frozen plain data showing state, memory, context, issues, lifecycle, schedules and
        operations.

        A snapshot exposes state (complete leaf path), memory (decoded value; fragment slices under
        their instance names, undefined while inactive and present while frozen, .each copies as a
        keyed list in key order, each with key), context (selected slots), issues (Diagnostic
        values), lifecycle (active, disposing or disposed; a persisted identity's record that does
        not decode reads quarantined, @plan.Runtime.O3), schedules (armed schedules with dueAt,
        keyed schedules as keyed lists) and operations (the operations' rows, @plan.Operations.O6;
        s.operations.<instance>.<name> for a fragment, a keyed list of copies under .each, each
        write row listing the lanes of that copy's path).

        Every list of several things is a keyed list in key order, each entry with key; an entry
        that does not exist is absent, never a default. Outside snapshot and view types omit
        Flow.private fields; Stories and Inspection still see them. It has no input, methods or
        public revision.

        Diagnostic values are @plan.Diagnostics.O1. Snapshots are deeply frozen plain data. A
        Runtime step publishes the bound lane rows it changes as a new snapshot; reading an old
        snapshot never consults live lanes.

        Unchanged parts retain identity across publications; Flow-owned containers are copied, while
        opaque application values retain identity. A later success or release clears its own
        source's issue, never another source's issue; a new actor incarnation inherits none.
        Disposal is @plan.Runtime.O6.
verification
    V1  Type tests prove inference and reject undeclared or foreign members, invalid guards,
        unfilled required ports, writes to machine input fields and fragment acting members used
        outside their fragment.
    V2  Tests prove construction rejects invalid declarations in order without starting work,
        including TwoDefaults, ImpureDefault, ReservedStateName and UnknownNotUncertain.
    V3  Stories prove self, re-entry as a new activation, descendant survival, parent/child work
        order, subroutine exits and abandonment, and history.
    V4  Tests prove the turn algorithm: before and after memory reads, atomic batches, fixed now,
        no-ops, refusal by a child and callback defect behavior.
    V5  Tests prove check agrees with handling and uses captured snapshots without effects.
    V6  Stories prove schedule order, armOn and disarmOn, keyed schedules, cancellation, debounce,
        overdue firing, waiting while unused and restore behavior.
    V7  Stories prove placement retention, release, the owner test, late turns and passive lane
        publication.
    V8  Stories prove reaction baselines, context triggers, event order and defect recovery.
    V9  Tests prove frozen captured snapshots, stable identities and source-specific issue clearing.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Internal identity encoding and construction validation machinery. Warning presentation.
        Schedule stepping implementation and inspection status fields. Issue indexing and diagnostic
        rendering details. Type-spike sizing and compiler performance measurements. Reflection
        behavior for proxies, getters, symbols and sparse arrays.
